WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.770
The best, most effective commercials leave
a lasting impression on the consumer — but

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:12.540
sometimes, brands take things too far … which
brings even more attention to the commercial,

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:17.490
and thus the brand, encouraging envelope-pushing
in the industry.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:22.289
Here are a few examples of banned commercial
spots from across the globe.

00:00:22.289 --> 00:00:23.890
Bar gets barred

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:29.380
Israeli supermodel Bar Refaeli has starred
in two commercials deemed too steamy for prime

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.089
time in her home country.

00:00:31.089 --> 00:00:36.750
One for clothing company Hoodies in 2016 is
basically a Sports Illustrated swimsuit issue

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.980
spread come-to-life.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:44.120
But the original cut lingered too long on
Bar's behind and implied she was bottomless

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:48.910
at one point, so it was censored by Israel's
version of the FCC.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:54.810
But even the censored version was too much:
the tamer spot still couldn't air until after

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:55.810
10 p.m.

00:00:55.810 --> 00:01:01.390
This wasn't the first time a Bar-starring
spot got barred from prime time: In 2014,

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:06.120
she co-starred in another Hoodies commercial
with a mustachioed Muppet … and two other

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.720
versions of herself … which was said to
have “too many sexual insinuations” …

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.480
“You just can’t enough of me, huh?”

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:15.780
Giving up the gun

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:21.400
In 2005, Microsoft cooked up a commercial
they knew would cause a commotion, so they

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:27.150
effectively "pre-banned" it in the US, allowing
only a limited audience of "hardcore" gamers

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:28.190
to see it.

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:34.400
The playful Xbox 360 spot featuring strangers
engaging in a sprawling finger-gun shoot-out

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:39.740
in the middle of a busy terminal never aired
on television and was only shown at promotional

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.020
events.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:41.620
“Boom!

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:42.620
Boom!”

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:48.540
“Ah!”

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:55.040
Once the ad leaked, the Internet speculated
wildly about why the spot wasn't given a wider

00:01:55.050 --> 00:02:01.030
release, leading to rumors about network or
FCC bans over its violent content … but

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:06.360
it was simply Microsoft thinking outside the
Xbox.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.980
No apologies

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:14.100
In a weird twist in 2014, a SodaStream Super
Bowl spot featuring Scarlett Johansson about

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:19.850
what it takes for a video to go viral ended
up going viral … but for an entirely different

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:22.370
reason than the one depicted in the video.

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:27.320
The spot showcases how easy it is to use the
device to create a soda that's better for

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:33.170
the consumer and the environment … but then
ScarJo decides that stripping down to a slinky

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:38.300
dress and sipping on her soda seductively
is what's needed to achieve viral stardom

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:39.360
"Sorry, Coke and Pepsi."

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.480
"Oh yeah, she done it.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:41.980
SodaStream!"

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:47.980
Ironically, the ploy wasn't even given a chance
to work, since FOX rejected the spot over

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:50.880
the mocking apology to SodaStream's competitors.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.170
But the controversy drew millions of eyeballs
to the "uncensored" spot with the offending

00:02:56.170 --> 00:03:01.130
line ... as well as the otherwise identical
"censored" spot during the big game.

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.500
Sorry, Coke and Pepsi.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.010
Dead to rights

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:10.650
This 2001 Dutch commercial for the Hyundai
Veloster featured the personification of Death

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:21.500
itself coming to claim a soul … and the
shockingly brutal way Death gets it done ...

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:22.700
Ouch.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:27.790
It's a good thing the Veloster only has three
doors, a Death-defying safety feature meant

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:29.730
to cut down on such accidents.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:33.880
Interestingly, the spooky spot was actually
shot down by Hyundai itself before airing

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.680
for being "too shocking" for their brand.

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:42.000
But like a lot of these banned ads, it lives
on in infamy.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:43.430
Trap house

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:48.630
Speaking of disturbingly violent images, how
about this shocking 2006 commercial from New

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:51.099
Zealand for the Toyota RAV4?

00:03:51.099 --> 00:03:56.910
The car is so amazing, apparently, that it
turns husband and wife against each other

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.819
in their shared quest to drive it.

00:03:58.819 --> 00:04:04.560
They set cartoonish, Home Alone-style traps
to try to slow the other down while they go

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.349
through their morning routine.

00:04:06.349 --> 00:04:12.370
Viewers complained it "sent an inappropriate
message to children," was "not socially responsible,"

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:17.139
and it "would encourage similar behavior"
… so the popular ad was yanked from the

00:04:17.139 --> 00:04:18.139
airwaves.

00:04:18.139 --> 00:04:20.019
For heaven's sake

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:27.949
This slick 2011 Axe Excite spot features skimpily-dressed
angels falling from heaven and giving up their

00:04:27.949 --> 00:04:32.300
immortality to hook up with a dude that wears
a spray-on deodorant.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.080
“Mama mia.”

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:41.180
The supposedly sacrilegious TV spot was banned
by South Africa's Advertising Standards Authority

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:46.300
for "offending members of the Christian faith"
… a move that surely increased the number

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:51.919
of curious Christians watching the spot online
— a devil of a predicament for censors.

00:04:51.919 --> 00:04:54.150
Indecent exposure

00:04:54.150 --> 00:05:00.290
In 2007, Bud Light created a cheeky commercial
featuring a carefree couple tossing off their

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:03.900
clothes and going skinny-dipping, assuming
they're alone ...

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:05.760
“Bartender!

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:06.689
Bud Light!”

00:05:06.689 --> 00:05:07.689
Oops.

00:05:07.689 --> 00:05:13.289
The spot was supposed to air during the Super
Bowl that year, but was rejected by CBS.

00:05:13.289 --> 00:05:19.189
The ban, however, led to even more exposure
for Budweiser, since it made headlines, generating

00:05:19.189 --> 00:05:24.770
tens of millions of views online, and giving
it a much longer shelf life — like being

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:28.069
featured in videos like this one, ten years
later.

00:05:28.069 --> 00:05:29.069
Thanks for watching!

